{"chunk_id": "faa7394be67c", "text": "This episode recounts the event that sets in motion King Parikshit\u2019s fatal curse. While hunting in the forest, the Kuru king wounds a deer and pursues it until he becomes exhausted and thirsty. He encounters the sage Samika (unnamed here but implied) sitting silently in meditation and asks about the deer. Because the sage is observing a vow of silence, he does not respond. Irritated and fatigued, Parikshit places a dead snake on the sage\u2019s shoulders as an insult, but soon regrets his act and departs. The sage himself forgives the king, recognizing it as a mistake. However, the sage\u2019s fiery young son Sringin hears of the insult from another boy and, inflamed with anger and pride in his ascetic power, curses Parikshit to die within seven days from the bite of the serpent king Takshaka. The moment illustrates the dangers of anger, the power of ascetic speech, and the fragile relationship between kings and sages.\nKing Parikshit insults a silent sage by placing a dead snake on him; the sage\u2019s son Sringin angrily curses the king to die by serpent Takshaka.\nThemes: royal arrogance, ascetic power, curse and fate, anger and restraint, brahmin\u2013king tension", "vector": [0.022971132770180702, 0.003616963978856802, -0.005517576355487108, -0.0048010079190135, -0.00898781418800354, 0.004708877764642239, -0.01838509552180767, -0.017402373254299164, -0.006240969058126211, -0.02766636200249195, 0.012754916213452816, -0.00016229419270530343, 0.02112852968275547, 0.005575584247708321, 0.0029259873554110527, 0.004108325112611055, 0.04878124222159386, -0.004811244551092386, 0.04285760968923569, -0.01373081374913454, 0.00132565142121166, 0.008653415367007256, 0.005793966818600893, 0.02174273133277893, -0.0044324868358671665, 0.022097602486610413, -0.0040093702264130116, -0.02130596525967121, 0.008223474957048893, -0.029454370960593224, -0.013846829533576965, -0.015109354630112648, 0.00038238303386606276, -0.00413221074268221